course: dictionary
lesson: 15479
title: Strep: a short word for a long name
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Strep is a short word that people use for a longer one. It is short for streptococcus, a kind of bacteria. Bacteria are tiny living things, too small to see, and some of them can make us ill.
explain: Strep is informal, so you will hear it in everyday talk, often in the phrase «strep throat». You do not say two stREPs. Strep is a word for the bacteria and for the illness they cause.
explain: Strep throat is a sore throat caused by these bacteria. It can bring a fever and pain when you swallow. A doctor may treat it with antibiotics, which are medicines that fight bacteria.
fact: Strep is short for streptococcus, a kind of bacteria.
fact: Bacteria are tiny living things, and some kinds can cause illness.
fact: «Strep throat» is a sore throat caused by these bacteria, often with a fever and pain when swallowing.
fact: Doctors may treat strep with antibiotics.
fact: In the USA and Canada, people often say «a strep test», where a cotton swab is rubbed at the back of the throat. In the UK people often say «a throat infection» or «tonsillitis».
exercise: «Mia had a sore throat and a fever, so the doctor gave her a strep test.» What is strep short for? | Stripes | *Streptococcus | Strawberry | Stretching
exercise: Which of these is a sign of strep throat? | Tiny freckles | Long hair | *Pain when swallowing | A broken arm
exercise: Which sentence about strep is true? | *It is short for streptococcus, a kind of bacteria | It is a kind of sunshine | It is a kind of food | It is a kind of bicycle
test: Why do people say «strep» instead of streptococcus? | Because it means a sunny day | *Because it is a shorter, everyday word | Because it is a type of medicine | Because it is a bird
test: Which treatment does a doctor sometimes give for strep? | Lots of ice cream | Only a long sleep | A new bicycle | *Antibiotics
exam: «Ella said, "Wash your hands and don't share cups, or strep can spread."» What helps to stop strep spreading? | *Hand-washing | Sneezing on the bus | Sharing cups with friends | Coughing into the air
